{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-4718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${F}^{′}(0)$ if $F(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-4718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\sqrt[3]{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\sqrt[3]{1+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}}-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\sqrt[3]{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${F}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\sqrt[3]{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(0)}-\\sqrt[3]{1+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\sqrt[3]{1}-\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-4718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "393fa87bf245c674fd56de7ed050e04de29d2b90ada2bc0b45f186cd517a0cf9"} {"question" "q-4718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-4718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\sqrt[3]{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt[3]{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-4718" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-75221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-x.$ Find ${g}^{′}(2)$ given the function is defined by $g(x)={\\int }_{x}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-75221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\int }_{x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-t)\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{0}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{0}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{0}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 2)-2) \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-75221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "393fa87bf245c674fd56de7ed050e04de29d2b90ada2bc0b45f186cd517a0cf9"} {"question" "q-75221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-75221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{g}^{′}(2)=-1"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(2)=-2"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(2)=0"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(2)=1"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(2)=2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-75221" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{g}^{′}(2)=2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={x}^{2}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[0,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})\\cdot u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{u} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot {x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "393fa87bf245c674fd56de7ed050e04de29d2b90ada2bc0b45f186cd517a0cf9"} {"question" "q-116501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{x}"] ["math" "2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{x}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=3x-5$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of the definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2u-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x-5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2u-1)) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(3x-5)-1) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "393fa87bf245c674fd56de7ed050e04de29d2b90ada2bc0b45f186cd517a0cf9"} {"question" "q-116504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)"] ["math" "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11)"] ["math" "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-11)"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and ${3}^{x}+2.$\n\nThe integrand $(6t-7)$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(6x-7)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =7-6x+({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6u-7) \\\\  & =7-6x+({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6({3}^{x}+2)-7) \\\\  & =7-6x+({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6\\cdot {3}^{x}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u={3}^{x}+2.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =7-6(0)+({3}^{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6\\cdot {3}^{0}+5) \\\\  & =7+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/state.json" "file_sha256" "7506666b12cf7c02c3dccb30b5a00fdedbf3f7257adb001de1c0a34b17bed204"} {"question" "q-116548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha25